Maps
====

Astir draws vector maps well and raster maps adequately, which is the reverse
of the usual priority and is deliberate: a vector map stays sharp at any zoom,
costs kilobytes rather than megabytes, and does not need a tile server standing
by.  For a program somebody carries to a place with no network, that matters
more than photographic detail.


What Astir can read
-------------------

  ESRI shapefiles       The main event.  Styled per feature by dbfawk rules
                        (below), drawn as real lines and fills.
  PMTiles               A whole region's vector tiles in one file, with no
                        server.  Read directly.
  Mapbox Vector Tiles   The tile format inside PMTiles, also readable loose.
  GeoTIFF               Georeferenced TIFF, such as USGS topographic sheets.
                        Needs libgeotiff and libproj.
  .geo images           Any raster image plus a .geo file giving its corners.
                        Needs GraphicsMagick or ImageMagick.
  OSM tile servers      Slippy-map tiles fetched over HTTP and cached.  Needs
                        libcurl.  Templates ship in maps/Online.
  WMS                   Web Map Service layers, also over HTTP.
  GNIS                  US place-name gazetteer files, drawn as labels.
  DOS-era formats       Astir's inherited .map format and APRSdos maps.

Which of these you get depends on what was compiled in.  `./configure` prints a
summary; read it rather than guessing.  See INSTALL.md.


What ships with it
------------------

A Natural Earth basemap -- coastlines, countries, lakes and rivers -- installed
under `maps/NaturalEarth` and selected automatically on first run, so Astir
draws a real map on a machine that has never been online.

It is the 1:50,000,000 world set.  At a city zoom it can offer you a coastline
and very little else; that is what it is for, not a defect.  For street-level
detail you need to add maps.


Where maps live
---------------

Installed maps go under the data directory, normally
`/usr/local/share/astir/maps`.  Anything you add goes in the same place, or in
a subdirectory of it -- Astir walks the tree, so organising by region or by
source is fine and is a good idea once you have more than a handful.

The map index is built on first run and cached in
`~/.astir/config/map_index.sys`.  After adding or removing map files, rebuild it
from **Menu -> Maps -> Rebuild Map Index**, or delete that file and restart.

Which maps are drawn is chosen in **Menu -> Maps -> Choose Maps** (`Ctrl+M`) and
remembered in `~/.astir/config/selected_maps.sys`.  A map being present is not
the same as a map being selected; if something you installed does not appear,
check the chooser before suspecting the file.


Getting map data
----------------

**OpenStreetMap, converted to shapefiles.**  `tools/osm_import.sh` turns an OSM
extract into shapefiles Astir already knows how to draw, split into layers whose
DBF field signatures match the shipped dbfawk rules.  This is the best route to
street-level detail that works offline.  Extracts by region are available from
Geofabrik and others.

**US Census TIGER/Line.**  Roads, boundaries, water and place names for the
United States, as shapefiles, free.  The dbfawk rules in `config/` were written
against these, so they style correctly with no work.

**Natural Earth.**  Larger scales than the bundled 1:50m set are available and
drop in the same way.

**USGS topographic maps** as GeoTIFF, if you want raster topos.

Downloaded map collections are yours, not the project's -- keep them outside
the source tree.  A checkout is not a place to store hundreds of megabytes of
map data, and `.gitignore` is set up on that assumption.


dbfawk: how a shapefile gets its appearance
-------------------------------------------

A shapefile says where the features are.  It does not say that a highway should
be a thick orange line and a stream a thin blue one.  dbfawk rules do that.

Each rule file matches a DBF field signature -- the colon-joined list of field
names in the shapefile's attribute table -- and then, per record, sets colour,
line width, fill, label text, label font and the zoom range over which the
feature is drawn at all.  75 rule files ship in `config/`, mostly written for
TIGER and Natural Earth.

To style a shapefile Astir does not recognise, copy the closest existing rule,
change the signature line to match your file's fields, and adjust from there.
`astir_testdbfawk -f rule.dbfawk -d map.dbf` runs a rule against a shapefile's
attribute table and prints the values it decided on, which is much faster than
restarting the program to look at the map.  `-D <dir>` instead of `-f` makes it
pick the rule the way Astir would, which is how you find out that a different
file is matching your signature.

The same rules are used by Xastir, so rule files are portable between the two.
The files themselves are not shared -- Astir reads only its own copy under its
own data directory, and refuses to read another program's.


Levels of detail
----------------

Maps carry a zoom range, so a map is skipped entirely when the view is far
enough out that it would be an unreadable smear, or far enough in that it has
nothing to add.  This is set per feature by the dbfawk rules for shapefiles, and
per map in the chooser otherwise.  It is the main lever if drawing feels slow:
a world view trying to draw every street in a county is doing work no one can
see.
